# handsoff-loop Slice DF amendment — user-operable end-to-end launch workflow

2026-06-24. An owner-directed amendment to the sealed Slice DF (design-first
front stage) per docs/plans/handsoff-design-first-and-build-heartbeat.md §5
(AC.DF.7). Empirically motivated: a live rehearsal (rehearsal-2) built a
Tkinter GUI whose only way in was `python the_gui.py` in a terminal. The
sealed AC.DF.6 had made the design's OUTPUT visible (a wizard, not a CLI) —
but the LAUNCH path was still the exact technical task the target
non-technical user (a small-firm owner) cannot do. Visible output is
necessary but NOT sufficient: the whole path to STARTING and USING the tool
must be operable by the target user.

MOTIVE (prime directive, Lens 0). The user owns WHAT they want; loam owns
HOW; but the HOW must TERMINATE in something the user can actually INITIATE
and operate — no leaked developer tasks. A design whose only way in is "run
the script" has handed a developer task back to the user even if its output
is a nice screen. Rehearsal-2 surfaced this defect cheaply, before the room —
which is the rehearsal's purpose. This is AC.DF.6 one layer deeper.

Single-component fence on the workspace-bootstrap anchor (the handsoff-loop
family's seal anchor — source under framework/tools/handsoff-loop/, inside
the workspace-bootstrap seal-test's admitted framework/tools/ prefix; same
pattern as the Slice DF / Slice HB / AC.DF.6 seals).

Change (generative.py):

  - NEW: CandidateDesign gains two ADDITIVE fields — launch_mechanism (a
    short label naming HOW the user STARTS the tool: "web app opened by URL",
    "double-click app", "scheduled email report") + user_workflow (an
    ordered tuple of the LITERAL steps the target user personally takes to
    launch and use the tool). Both default empty, so every direct
    CandidateDesign(...) construction in the sealed DF tests stays valid (no
    positional break). as_evidence() surfaces both.
  - NEW: the per-candidate prompt asks for launch_mechanism + user_workflow;
    the non-tech prompt constraint demands an operable launcher (a browser
    URL/link, a double-click packaged app, or email/file delivery) with
    concrete steps and FORBIDS a terminal/script/dev-env launch — even when
    the output is a GUI.
  - NEW: is_launch_user_operable(launch_mechanism, user_workflow) — a
    held-out STRUCTURAL classifier (domain-blind, prompt-blind) that reads
    ONLY the launch-surface vocabulary (the label + the literal workflow
    steps) and DISQUALIFIES a launch requiring developer machinery to START
    the tool (a terminal, a script run like `python foo.py`, a dev-env
    install), and requires a recognised operable launcher (browser URL /
    double-click app / delivery). For a non-tech user the candidate loop
    applies this filter in the SAME accrue-time drop as is_nontech_operable,
    so the user is NEVER surfaced a candidate they cannot launch even if its
    OUTPUT is a GUI and even if the model ignores the prompt. The classifier
    never sees the generation prompt — it is the falsifiable AC.DF.7 check.
  - NEW: as_direction_brief() carries the chosen candidate's launch_mechanism
    + user_workflow into the buildable-design generation (the seam the chosen
    direction already flows through, build_from_intent.py L405-410), so the
    build is DIRECTED to target the committed launch form-factor (a "web app
    opened by URL" design conditions the build toward a browser-openable app,
    not a bare desktop script). A real linkage, not advisory.
  - UNCHANGED: the single-design generate_design path; the AC.GEN.2
    domain-blindness (the new launch vocabulary keys on interaction/launch
    surfaces, NOT any business domain — the rehearsal-2 example was scrubbed
    of "reconcile" from source, kept only in the AC.DF.7 test + the plan-doc);
    the choose_design_fn=None non-interactive S6 path (the additive
    empty-default fields keep it byte-preserved); the AC.DF.6
    is_nontech_operable classifier (AC.DF.7 layers ON it, does not replace
    it).

AC surface:

  - AC.DF.7 — every candidate design carries a user-operable end-to-end
    LAUNCH workflow; a non-tech user is never offered a candidate whose
    launch requires a terminal, a script run, or a dev-env, EVEN IF its
    output is a GUI. Verified by a held-out structural classifier the
    generator never saw + an outcome-altitude live test (env-gated) that runs
    a real generation over the rehearsal-2 ask and asserts every surfaced
    candidate carries a non-empty literal user_workflow that passes the
    held-out is_launch_user_operable classifier.

Test-fixture updates (same seal prefix): the AC.DF.1 + AC.DF.6 test
fixtures' candidates now carry an operable launch workflow (a real non-tech
candidate has one), so the tightened AC.DF.7 launch filter does not drop them
— the fixtures' form_factor values (which the AC.DF.1 distinctness assertions
key on) are untouched; only an operable launch_mechanism + user_workflow are
added. This is the correct interaction: AC.DF.7 tightens the non-tech bar, and
a fixture standing in for a real surfaced candidate must now state how the
user launches it.

Method-level choices are the builder's call per ODD §1.1: the two-layer
enforcement (prompt constraint at generation + held-out classifier at filter)
mirrors AC.DF.6 and gives the non-tech user a clean candidate set even if one
layer slips.

Build-stage scope (SAL-DF-4): the DESIGN-side guarantee + the build-target
directive (the chosen launch mechanism flows into the buildable-design
generation) close AC.DF.7. Making the build STAGE actually BUILD + VERIFY a
served, browser-openable web app — a served-app check contract distinct from
the current shell-script gate — is a substantial, independently-scoped
build-stage change SURFACED AS A FOLLOW-UP (§9 / a task), NOT built this
cycle, per the dispatch's explicit halt on substantial build-stage form-factor
work.

Out of scope: the build-stage served-web-app build + verification harness
(surfaced follow-up, SAL-DF-4); inferring the user's tech level upstream in
intake (the default non-tech covers the demo + the owner ruling); a clickable
mockup; changing the build / freeze / verify spine; pushing to origin
(owner-gated — LOCAL seal only).

